# Line-ending policy: this repository always stores LF.
#
# Why this file exists: the working tree lives on a synced folder (Google Drive /
# Syncthing), which rewrites text files to CRLF behind git's back. Without `text`
# attributes git compares raw bytes, so every synced file shows as wholly modified
# and real changes get buried in thousand-line whitespace diffs.
#
# With `text=auto` git normalizes to LF when staging and when diffing, so a CRLF
# working tree compares clean against an LF repository. `git add --renormalize .`
# was run once when this file was introduced, so committed blobs are LF regardless
# of what the working tree currently holds.
#
# Practical effect: `git status` stays quiet until you actually change something,
# and `git diff` shows only real edits. Nothing needs to be done per-checkout.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the real exception -- cmd.exe can mis-parse LF-only batch
# files (notably around labels and goto), so these keep CRLF in the working tree.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# `text=auto` detects binaries by looking for NUL bytes, which misses some
# formats. Mark the common ones explicitly so they are never line-ending munged.
